\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Building Trees with
\texttt{leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{node\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}}{Building Trees with  and }}\label{ch11-building-trees-with-leaf-and-node}

Chapter 10 introduced \texttt{ASTNode}, the value-type node that backs
every abstract syntax tree in MuDSL. Constructing those nodes by hand,
however, requires spelling out the tag as a run-time
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view},
choosing the correct constructor overload, and assembling the children
vector yourself. The library therefore provides two ergonomic factory
functions,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
that turn the common cases into one-line expressions. This chapter
documents those factories in detail.

Both factories share a single design idea: the tag is supplied as a
compile-time string literal in the template argument list, while the
run-time payload (a value or a list of children) is supplied as ordinary
function arguments. The result is an \texttt{ASTNode} prvalue that can
be immediately moved into a parent or bound to a variable. The factories
are the primary way MuDSL code builds trees, and every later chapter on
traversal, rewriting, and lazy evaluation assumes the trees were
assembled this way.

\section{11.1 The Role of the
Factories}\label{ch11-the-role-of-the-factories}

An \texttt{ASTNode} is, mechanically, a small class with a tag string, a
value string, a vector of children, and a boolean distinguishing leaves
from inner nodes. Its constructors accept
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag,\allowbreak{} value)\allowbreak{}} for leaves
and \texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag,\allowbreak{} children)\allowbreak{}} for
inner nodes. Nothing prevents a user from invoking those constructors
directly, and doing so is sometimes useful in generic code.

The factories exist because the direct form is verbose and error-prone
at call sites. The tag, which is almost always a literal known at
compile time, has to be wrapped in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} or a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} by hand. The children
vector has to be constructed and moved explicitly. The intent of the
code is buried under mechanics.

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}children.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
lift the tag into the template parameter list, where a string literal
can be written directly, and handle the assembly internally. They are
pure convenience functions with no hidden state. Their behavior is
entirely described by the definitions quoted in the next two sections.

\section{\texorpdfstring{11.2 The
\texttt{leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
Factory}{11.2 The  Factory}}\label{ch11-the-leaf-factory}

The \texttt{leaf} template constructs a leaf node. A leaf is a node with
a tag and a value but no children. Its definition, verbatim from the
header, is:

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Tag, typename T>
ASTNode
leaf (T &&val)
{
  std::ostringstream os;
  os << std::forward<T> (val);
  return ASTNode{ Tag.view (), os.str () };
}
\end{code}

The template has two parameters. The first, \texttt{Tag}, is a non-type
template parameter of type
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}FixedString} (Chapter 4). It is
deduced from a string literal written in the angle brackets. The second,
\texttt{T}, is the type of the value being stored and is deduced from
the function argument.

The body of the factory is simple. It feeds the value into an
\texttt{ostringstream}, which converts it to its textual form using
ordinary stream insertion. It then constructs an \texttt{ASTNode} via
the leaf constructor
\texttt{ASTNode(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view tag,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string value)\allowbreak{}},
passing \texttt{Tag.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} for
the tag and the resulting string for the value.

The use of \texttt{ostringstream} means any type with a stream insertion
operator can be stored directly. Integers, floating-point numbers,
booleans, and anything for which
\texttt{operator\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}} is
defined will be accepted.

\section{\texorpdfstring{11.3 String Overloads of
\texttt{leaf}}{11.3 String Overloads of }}\label{ch11-string-overloads-of-leaf}

Because the general template routes every value through an
\texttt{ostringstream}, the header also provides two overloads that skip
the stream for the common case where the value is already text. They
are:

\begin{code}
/// Overload for string_view values (avoids extra stream).
template <FixedString Tag>
ASTNode
leaf (std::string_view val)
{
  return ASTNode{ Tag.view (), std::string (val) };
}

/// Overload for const char* values.
template <FixedString Tag>
ASTNode
leaf (const char *val)
{
  return ASTNode{ Tag.view (), std::string (val) };
}
\end{code}

These overloads are exact matches for
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} and
\texttt{const char *} arguments, so overload resolution prefers them
over the general template. They construct the
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} value directly from the
argument, avoiding the cost of formatting through a stream.

From the caller's perspective the distinction is invisible. The same
syntax,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"name"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}argument)\allowbreak{}},
is used regardless of the argument type. What changes is only the
internal conversion path.

\section{11.4 A First Leaf Example}\label{ch11-a-first-leaf-example}

The smallest useful tree is a single leaf. The header's own overview
gives the canonical form:

\begin{code}
auto leaf_node = dsl::leaf<"name">(value);
\end{code}

Concretely, a numeric leaf is written as follows.

\begin{code}
auto n = dsl::leaf<"num">(42);
std::cout << n.dump() << "\n";   // prints (num 42)
\end{code}

The \texttt{dump} member function (Chapter 10) renders a leaf as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag value)\allowbreak{}}. Because \texttt{42} is
an \texttt{int}, the general template is selected, the integer is
streamed into the \texttt{ostringstream}, and the resulting string
\texttt{"42"} is stored as the leaf's value.

The same syntax accepts a string literal, in which case the
\texttt{const char *} overload is selected.

\begin{code}
auto id = dsl::leaf<"ident">("foo");
std::cout << id.dump() << "\n";  // prints (ident foo)
\end{code}

Either way, the produced node reports
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}leaf(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} == true}, has
an empty \texttt{children(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} vector, and
carries its payload in \texttt{value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

\section{\texorpdfstring{11.5 The
\texttt{node\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
Factory}{11.5 The  Factory}}\label{ch11-the-node-factory}

The \texttt{node} template constructs an inner node. An inner node has a
tag and a list of children but no value of its own. Its definition,
verbatim from the header, is:

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
  requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
ASTNode
node (Children &&...children)
{
  std::vector<ASTNode> v;
  v.reserve (sizeof...(children));
  (v.emplace_back (std::forward<Children> (children)), ...);
  return ASTNode{ Tag.view (), std::move (v) };
}
\end{code}

This is a variadic template. The \texttt{Tag} parameter is, as with
\texttt{leaf}, a \texttt{FixedString} non-type template parameter. The
\texttt{Children.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}} pack
captures the run-time arguments, which must each be convertible to
\texttt{ASTNode}.

The body builds a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}ASTNode\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
of the right capacity, then uses a fold expression to perfect-forward
each child into the vector via \texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}back}.
Finally it calls the inner-node constructor
\texttt{ASTNode(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view tag,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}ASTNode\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} children)\allowbreak{}},
moving the assembled vector into the new node.

The \texttt{requires} clause is the only constraint. Every argument type
must satisfy
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}convertible\_\allowbreak{}to\textless{}\allowbreak{}Children,\allowbreak{} ASTNode\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
This permits \texttt{ASTNode} prvalues (the usual case) as well as
anything that has a conversion to \texttt{ASTNode}.

\section{11.6 The Tag as a Non-Type Template
Parameter}\label{ch11-the-tag-as-a-non-type-template-parameter}

The most important design choice in both factories is that the tag is a
template argument, not a function argument. This is what allows the call
site to write the tag as a bare string literal inside the angle
brackets.

\begin{code}
dsl::node<"+">(lhs, rhs);
\end{code}

Here \texttt{"+"} is not an expression evaluated at run time. It is a
template argument, and the compiler deduces a \texttt{FixedString} value
from it. The \texttt{FixedString} class (Chapter 4) is a literal type
with a fixed maximum length, designed precisely so that it can be used
as a non-type template parameter.

Inside the factory,
\texttt{Tag.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} over
the fixed buffer, which the \texttt{ASTNode} constructor copies into its
own \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} member. The tag
therefore lives in two places: a compile-time \texttt{FixedString}
embedded in the type of the factory invocation, and a run-time
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} owned by the resulting
node.

This split is what makes the factories both type-safe and convenient.
The compiler verifies the tag at instantiation time, while the node
retains a plain \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} for
run-time inspection and comparison.

\section{11.7 Why the Tag Cannot Be a Run-Time
String}\label{ch11-why-the-tag-cannot-be-a-run-time-string}

Because the tag is a non-type template parameter, it must be a constant
expression. A string literal qualifies. A \texttt{const char *} variable
does not, even if it points at a literal, because its value is not part
of the type system.

\begin{code}
// OK: literal in the template argument list
auto a = dsl::leaf<"num">(1);

// Ill-formed: 'name' is not a constant expression
const char* name = "num";
auto b = dsl::leaf<name>(1);   // error
\end{code}

The same restriction applies to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} objects, which are not
literal types and therefore cannot be non-type template parameters at
all. Code that needs to choose a tag at run time must fall back to
constructing an \texttt{ASTNode} directly, passing the tag to the
constructor.

This is an intentional trade-off. The factory syntax is optimized for
the common case where the tag is a fixed, known label such as
\texttt{"num"}, \texttt{"add"}, or \texttt{"if"}. Tags chosen at run
time are rare in MuDSL usage, and the direct constructor remains
available for them.

\section{\texorpdfstring{11.8 Children Must Be Convertible to
\texttt{ASTNode}}{11.8 Children Must Be Convertible to }}\label{ch11-children-must-be-convertible-to-astnode}

The \texttt{node} factory places no constraint on the tag, but it
constrains its arguments. The \texttt{requires} clause demands that
every child type satisfy
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}convertible\_\allowbreak{}to\textless{}\allowbreak{}Children,\allowbreak{} ASTNode\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
In practice this means each argument must be an \texttt{ASTNode} or
something that converts to one.

The most common argument type is, of course, an \texttt{ASTNode} prvalue
returned by another \texttt{leaf} or \texttt{node} call. The conversion
is trivial in that case. An lvalue \texttt{ASTNode} also works; it will
be copy-constructed into the vector.

\begin{code}
auto lhs = dsl::leaf<"num">(1);
auto rhs = dsl::leaf<"num">(2);
auto add = dsl::node<"+">(lhs, rhs);   // copies lhs and rhs
\end{code}

Types that are not convertible to \texttt{ASTNode} are rejected at
compile time. There is no implicit conversion from, say, \texttt{int} to
\texttt{ASTNode}, so passing a bare integer as a child is an error.

\begin{code}
auto bad = dsl::node<"+">(1, 2);   // error: int is not convertible to ASTNode
\end{code}

The fix is to wrap each scalar in a \texttt{leaf} first. The factories
do not guess how to promote a value to a node; the user states the tag
explicitly.

\section{11.9 A First Inner Node}\label{ch11-a-first-inner-node}

The header's overview gives the canonical two-child form:

\begin{code}
auto inner_node = dsl::node<"op">(child1, child2);
\end{code}

Combined with two leaves, this produces the smallest non-trivial tree.

\begin{code}
auto expr = dsl::node<"add">(
    dsl::leaf<"num">(1),
    dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
std::cout << expr.dump() << "\n";   // (add (num 1) (num 2))
\end{code}

This is the exact example from the header's AST overview. The resulting
node has
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}leaf(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} == false},
\texttt{tag(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} == "add"},
\texttt{size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} == 2}, and
\texttt{child(\allowbreak{}0)\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{child(\allowbreak{}1)\allowbreak{}} are the two numeric leaves.

The order of the children is preserved exactly as written. The
\texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}back} fold expression processes the pack
left to right, so the first argument becomes
\texttt{child(\allowbreak{}0)\allowbreak{}}, the second becomes
\texttt{child(\allowbreak{}1)\allowbreak{}}, and so on. Ordering is
significant for most ASTs and is guaranteed.

\section{11.10 Variadic Arity}\label{ch11-variadic-arity}

The \texttt{node} factory accepts any number of children, including
zero. Each additional child is simply another argument in the pack.

\begin{code}
auto unary  = dsl::node<"neg">(dsl::leaf<"num">(4));
auto binary = dsl::node<"+">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
auto ternary = dsl::node<"if">(
    dsl::leaf<"bool">(true),
    dsl::leaf<"num">(1),
    dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
\end{code}

A node with zero children is also valid. It is constructed as an inner
node with an empty vector, distinct from a leaf.

\begin{code}
auto empty = dsl::node<"block">();
std::cout << empty.dump() << "\n";   // (block)
std::cout << empty.is_leaf() << "\n"; // 0
\end{code}

This form is occasionally useful for placeholder nodes such as empty
statement blocks, where the absence of children is meaningful but the
node is still structurally an inner node.

\section{11.11 Composing Trees by
Nesting}\label{ch11-composing-trees-by-nesting}

Because both factories return \texttt{ASTNode} prvalues, they compose by
nesting. A \texttt{node} call can take further \texttt{node} calls as
arguments, which in turn can take \texttt{leaf} or \texttt{node} calls,
to arbitrary depth.

\begin{code}
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(
    dsl::node<"*">(
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("a"),
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("b")),
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("c"));
std::cout << t.dump() << "\n";
// (+ (* (ident a) (ident b)) (ident c))
\end{code}

The whole tree is built in a single expression. No temporary variables
are required, though they may be used for readability. The \texttt{dump}
output is a flat S-expression that preserves the nesting.

This compositional style is the idiomatic way to build ASTs in MuDSL. It
mirrors the structure of the source language directly: an expression is
a node whose children are subexpressions, all the way down to
identifiers and literals.

\section{11.12 Move Semantics and
Prvalues}\label{ch11-move-semantics-and-prvalues}

The factories return \texttt{ASTNode} by value. When a factory call
appears as an argument to another factory, the inner prvalue is
materialized and then moved (or copy-elided) into the parent's vector.
The \texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}back} call in \texttt{node} takes a
forwarding reference, so an rvalue argument is moved into the vector
rather than copied.

\begin{code}
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(
    dsl::leaf<"num">(1),   // prvalue, moved into the vector
    dsl::leaf<"num">(2));  // prvalue, moved into the vector
\end{code}

For lvalue arguments the situation is different. An lvalue
\texttt{ASTNode} is copy-constructed into the vector, because
\texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}back} on an lvalue reference invokes the
copy constructor.

\begin{code}
auto a = dsl::leaf<"num">(1);
auto b = dsl::leaf<"num">(2);
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(a, b);   // a and b are copied
// a and b are still valid here
\end{code}

Both forms are correct. The prvalue form avoids a copy; the lvalue form
keeps the originals available for later use. MuDSL's value-semantics
design (Chapter 10) means either choice is safe.

\section{\texorpdfstring{11.13 The \texttt{AST} Feature
Tag}{11.13 The  Feature Tag}}\label{ch11-the-ast-feature-tag}

For DSLs that derive from \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL},
the library provides an \texttt{AST} feature tag whose mixin exposes the
same factories as static member functions. Its definition is:

\begin{code}
struct AST
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <FixedString Tag, typename T>
    static ASTNode
    make_leaf (T &&val)
    {
      return dsl::leaf<Tag> (std::forward<T> (val));
    }

    template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
      requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
    static ASTNode
    make_node (Children &&...children)
    {
      return dsl::node<Tag> (std::forward<Children> (children)...);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

The mixin simply forwards to the free functions. A DSL that mixes in
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}AST} gains
\texttt{MyDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}make\_\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{MyDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}make\_\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
as aliases for the free functions.

\begin{code}
struct MyDSL : dsl::DSL<MyDSL, dsl::AST> {};
auto t = MyDSL::make_node<"root">(MyDSL::make_leaf<"val">(42));
\end{code}

The feature tag is purely a naming convenience. It collects the AST
builders under the DSL's own scope, which can improve readability in
larger programs that import several DSLs. The behavior is identical to
calling \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node} directly.

\section{11.14 Building an Arithmetic
Tree}\label{ch11-building-an-arithmetic-tree}

The arithmetic expression
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}+ (\allowbreak{}* a b)\allowbreak{} c)\allowbreak{}}
is a classic two-level tree. With the factories it is written almost as
it reads.

\begin{code}
auto expr = dsl::node<"+">(
    dsl::node<"*">(
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("a"),
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("b")),
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("c"));
std::cout << expr.dump() << "\n";
// (+ (* (ident a) (ident b)) (ident c))
\end{code}

The outer \texttt{+} node has two children. The first child is itself a
\texttt{*} node with two identifier leaves. The second child is a single
identifier leaf. The structure of the call mirrors the structure of the
expression.

Tags here are short operator-like strings. There is nothing special
about them; they are ordinary \texttt{FixedString} values chosen by
convention. A DSL is free to use \texttt{"add"}, \texttt{"plus"}, or any
other label, as long as the same label is used consistently by the code
that inspects the tree.

\section{11.15 Building an Assignment
Tree}\label{ch11-building-an-assignment-tree}

An assignment such as \texttt{x :\allowbreak{}= x + 1} mixes a variable
on the left and an expression on the right. The tree is naturally
binary.

\begin{code}
auto assign = dsl::node<":=">(
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("x"),
    dsl::node<"+">(
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("x"),
        dsl::leaf<"num">(1)));
std::cout << assign.dump() << "\n";
// (:= (ident x) (+ (ident x) (num 1)))
\end{code}

The \texttt{:\allowbreak{}=} node carries no value of its own. Its
meaning is entirely structural: the first child is the target, the
second is the value to store. A later rewrite pass (Chapter 13) could
match on the tag \texttt{":\allowbreak{}="} and transform the children
accordingly.

The identifier \texttt{x} appears twice in this tree, once as a leaf on
the left and once inside the addition. The two leaves are independent
\texttt{ASTNode} objects that happen to carry the same text. There is no
sharing of nodes; value semantics forbid it.

\section{11.16 Building a Function Call
Tree}\label{ch11-building-a-function-call-tree}

A function call
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}call f arg1 arg2\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} has
variable arity. The \texttt{node} factory handles this directly, since
the children pack may be any length.

\begin{code}
auto call = dsl::node<"call">(
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("f"),
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("arg1"),
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("arg2"));
std::cout << call.dump() << "\n";
// (call (ident f) (ident arg1) (ident arg2))
\end{code}

By convention the first child is the function being called and the
remaining children are the arguments. The factory itself enforces no
such convention; it merely preserves order. The interpretation is up to
the code that consumes the tree.

A call with no arguments is simply a \texttt{call} node with one child.

\begin{code}
auto nullary = dsl::node<"call">(dsl::leaf<"ident">("f"));
// (call (ident f))
\end{code}

Either form is built with the same syntax, differing only in how many
children are supplied.

\section{11.17 Building a Conditional
Tree}\label{ch11-building-a-conditional-tree}

An \texttt{if} expression with a condition, a then-branch, and an
else-branch is a three-child node. Mixed children, some leaves and some
inner nodes, are perfectly acceptable.

\begin{code}
auto if_tree = dsl::node<"if">(
    dsl::node<"<">(
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("x"),
        dsl::leaf<"num">(0)),
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("neg"),
    dsl::leaf<"ident">("pos"));
std::cout << if_tree.dump() << "\n";
// (if (< (ident x) (num 0)) (ident neg) (ident pos))
\end{code}

The condition is itself an inner node comparing \texttt{x} to zero. The
two branches are identifier leaves. A tree like this could be the input
to a simplifier that, for example, propagates a known sign of \texttt{x}
and rewrites the conditional to one of its branches (Chapter 13).

Mixed construction is the rule rather than the exception. Real ASTs
almost always combine leaves for atoms and inner nodes for compound
forms, and the factories place no restriction on the mixture.

\section{11.18 A Larger Program Example}\label{ch11-a-larger-program-example}

Putting the pieces together, a small straight-line program can be
assembled as a single tree. The example below builds a \texttt{block}
containing an assignment and a return.

\begin{code}
auto program = dsl::node<"block">(
    dsl::node<":=">(
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("x"),
        dsl::node<"+">(
            dsl::leaf<"ident">("x"),
            dsl::leaf<"num">(1))),
    dsl::node<"return">(
        dsl::leaf<"ident">("x")));
std::cout << program.dump() << "\n";
// (block (:= (ident x) (+ (ident x) (num 1))) (return (ident x)))
\end{code}

The \texttt{block} node has two children, processed in order. A
traversal (Chapter 12) walking this tree would visit the assignment
first and the return second, which is the intended program order.

This example also illustrates how the factories scale. There is no
special syntax for ``a program''; a program is simply a \texttt{block}
node whose children are statements, which are themselves nodes. The same
two factories suffice for trees of any size.

\section{11.19 Value Semantics
Reaffirmed}\label{ch11-value-semantics-reaffirmed}

Every tree built in this chapter is a value. The factories return
\texttt{ASTNode} prvalues, and binding them to an \texttt{auto} variable
copies or moves the node as appropriate. There is no hidden allocation
beyond what \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector} and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} already perform, and no
reference counting.

Because nodes are values, a tree can be copied freely. Copying a node
copies its tag, its value, and recursively all of its children. Two
copies of a tree are independent: modifying one (by mutating its
\texttt{children(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} vector, for example) does
not affect the other.

\begin{code}
auto a = dsl::node<"+">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
auto b = a;                       // deep copy
b.children().push_back(dsl::leaf<"num">(3));
// a still has two children; b now has three
\end{code}

This is the same value-semantics contract described in Chapter 10. The
factories do not alter it; they merely produce the values that
participate in it.

\section{11.20 Why Factories over Direct
Construction}\label{ch11-why-factories-over-direct-construction}

It is worth pausing to compare the factory form with the equivalent
direct construction. The same two-child addition built directly is:

\begin{code}
dsl::ASTNode add{"+",
    std::vector<dsl::ASTNode>{
        dsl::ASTNode{"num", "1"},
        dsl::ASTNode{"num", "2"}}};
\end{code}

This is correct but clumsy. The tag is written as a string literal at
run time, the children vector is spelled out explicitly, and each child
must be constructed with its tag and value as positional arguments. The
intent of the code is obscured by the machinery.

The factory form restores the intent.

\begin{code}
auto add = dsl::node<"+">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
\end{code}

The tag is in the template argument list, where it visually belongs. The
children are simply listed. The leaf values are accepted in their
natural types. Brevity and clarity are the only reasons to prefer the
factories, but they are sufficient reasons in practice.

\section{11.21 Pitfall: Run-Time Tags}\label{ch11-pitfall-run-time-tags}

The most common mistake with the factories is attempting to pass a
run-time string as the tag. Because the tag is a non-type template
parameter, this fails to compile.

\begin{code}
std::string tag = "add";
auto t = dsl::node<tag>(...);   // error: 'tag' is not a constant expression
\end{code}

The fix is either to use a literal in the template argument list or, if
the tag genuinely varies at run time, to construct the \texttt{ASTNode}
directly.

\begin{code}
dsl::ASTNode t{tag, std::vector<dsl::ASTNode>{...}};
\end{code}

The direct constructor is the escape hatch for cases the factories do
not cover. It is not deprecated or discouraged; it is simply not the
primary interface.

\section{11.22 Pitfall: Wrong Argument Types for
Children}\label{ch11-pitfall-wrong-argument-types-for-children}

A second common mistake is passing a non-\texttt{ASTNode} argument to
\texttt{node}. The \texttt{requires} clause rejects this at compile
time, but the diagnostic can be cryptic.

\begin{code}
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(1, 2);   // error: int is not convertible to ASTNode
\end{code}

The factory does not infer a tag for bare values. Each value must be
wrapped in a \texttt{leaf} call that names its tag explicitly.

\begin{code}
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
\end{code}

This is a deliberate design choice. Tags are part of the meaning of a
node, and the library refuses to guess them.

\section{11.23 Pitfall: Ordering of
Children}\label{ch11-pitfall-ordering-of-children}

A third pitfall is assuming the factories sort or deduplicate children.
They do not. Children are stored in the order given, duplicates and all.

\begin{code}
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(
    dsl::leaf<"num">(1),
    dsl::leaf<"num">(1));
// two distinct children, both (num 1)
\end{code}

For an addition this is harmless. For a node where order is semantically
meaningful, such as an assignment
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}= target value)\allowbreak{}},
swapping the children produces a different and probably incorrect tree.
The factories preserve order faithfully; correctness is the caller's
responsibility.

\section{11.24 Combining with Traversal}\label{ch11-combining-with-traversal}

The trees built by \texttt{leaf} and \texttt{node} are the input to
every later stage of a DSL pipeline. Chapter 12 documents the traversal
helpers that walk a tree and the \texttt{dump} function that renders it.
The S-expression output shown throughout this chapter is produced by
\texttt{ASTNode:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}dump}, which the factories'
output supports directly.

\begin{code}
auto t = dsl::node<"+">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), dsl::leaf<"num">(2));
t.dump();   // "(+ (num 1) (num 2))"
\end{code}

A traversal that needs to visit every leaf, for example, recurses
through \texttt{children(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and stops at
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}leaf(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
factories guarantee the structure that such a traversal expects: leaves
have empty \texttt{children(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, inner nodes
have empty \texttt{value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

\section{11.25 Combining with Rewriting}\label{ch11-combining-with-rewriting}

Chapter 13 documents the rewrite system, which matches patterns against
trees and replaces them with transformed trees. The input to a rewrite
is always an \texttt{ASTNode} built by these factories, and the output
is another \texttt{ASTNode} built the same way.

The rewrite example from the library's test suite constructs a
double-negation tree using the factories directly.

\begin{code}
auto in = dsl::node<"neg">(dsl::node<"neg">(dsl::leaf<"num">(4)));
auto out = rules.apply(in);
std::cout << out.dump() << "\n";   // (num 4)
\end{code}

The \texttt{neg} nodes and the \texttt{num} leaf are all built with the
factories documented here. The rewrite system itself is the subject of
later chapters; the point for now is that the factories are the
universal entry point for tree construction, whether the tree is an
input, an intermediate, or an output.

\section{11.26 Choosing Tags}\label{ch11-choosing-tags}

The factories place no constraints on the tag string beyond the limits
of \texttt{FixedString} (Chapter 4). Tags may be operator-like
(\texttt{"+"}, \texttt{":\allowbreak{}="},
\texttt{"\textless{}\allowbreak{}"}) or word-like (\texttt{"add"},
\texttt{"assign"}, \texttt{"lt"}). The choice is a matter of convention
for each DSL.

A consistent convention pays off when the tree is inspected later. A
rewrite rule that matches on
\texttt{tag(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} == "add"} will miss nodes tagged
\texttt{"+"}, so the convention should be agreed upon before the tree is
built and applied uniformly.

Short tags keep \texttt{dump} output compact, which is useful during
debugging. Word-like tags are more self-documenting and may be preferred
in larger trees. Neither choice affects performance; the tag is a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} inside the node
regardless.

\section{11.27 Building Trees from Parser
Output}\label{ch11-building-trees-from-parser-output}

In practice, trees are rarely written out by hand as in the examples
above. More often they are constructed by a parser that has recognized a
piece of input and emits the corresponding node. The factories are
equally suited to this case.

A parser combinator (Chapter 23) that recognizes an integer literal can
emit a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"num"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}
in its semantic action. A combinator that recognizes a binary expression
can emit a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}op\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}left,\allowbreak{} right)\allowbreak{}}
from the values produced by its sub-parsers. Because the factories are
ordinary functions, they compose naturally with the parser combinator
framework.

The same principle applies to the PEG matcher of Chapter 28. Whatever
the source of the recognized text, the resulting tree is assembled with
the same two factories.

\section{11.28 A Note on Performance}\label{ch11-a-note-on-performance}

The factories are thin wrappers around the \texttt{ASTNode}
constructors. A \texttt{leaf} call performs one stream insertion (or one
string copy, for the string overloads) and one \texttt{ASTNode}
construction. A \texttt{node} call performs one vector allocation, a
sequence of \texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}back} calls, and one
\texttt{ASTNode} construction.

The vector allocation is the most significant cost. The
\texttt{reserve(\allowbreak{}sizeof.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}children)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
call ensures a single allocation sized exactly to the number of
children, so there is no reallocation as the pack is expanded. For very
large trees the dominant cost remains the recursive construction of
children, which is inherent in the tree's size.

There is no allocator customization in the factories. Nodes use the
default allocator for \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector} and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}. Code with specialized
allocation needs should construct \texttt{ASTNode} objects directly.

\section{11.29 Interoperability with Direct
Construction}\label{ch11-interoperability-with-direct-construction}

The factories and the direct constructors produce identical
\texttt{ASTNode} objects. A tree can mix the two freely, with some nodes
built by factories and others constructed directly.

\begin{code}
dsl::ASTNode direct{"num", "0"};
auto wrapped = dsl::node<"+">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), direct);
// (+ (num 1) (num 0))
\end{code}

Here the first child is built by \texttt{leaf} and the second by the
direct constructor. The \texttt{+} node built by \texttt{node} accepts
both, because both are \texttt{ASTNode} values. The \texttt{requires}
clause is satisfied by the direct constructor's result just as it is by
a factory's result.

This interoperability means the factories can be adopted incrementally.
Existing code that constructs nodes directly need not be rewritten; new
code can use the factories, and the two will combine without friction.

\section{11.30 Summary}\label{ch11-summary}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}children.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
are the primary constructors for \texttt{ASTNode} trees. They lift the
tag into the template argument list as a \texttt{FixedString} non-type
template parameter (Chapter 4) and accept the run-time payload as
ordinary function arguments.

\texttt{leaf} produces a leaf node: a tag plus a textual value, with no
children. Its general overload streams the value through an
\texttt{ostringstream}, so any type with a stream insertion operator is
accepted. Two overloads handle
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} and
\texttt{const char *} directly, skipping the stream.

\texttt{node} produces an inner node: a tag plus a vector of children,
with no value. It is variadic, accepting any number of children subject
to the constraint that each type be convertible to \texttt{ASTNode}.
Children are stored in the order given, via a fold expression over
\texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}back}.

Both factories return \texttt{ASTNode} prvalues, so trees compose by
nesting and move into their parents without copying. The \texttt{AST}
feature tag exposes the same factories as
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}leaf} and \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}node}
member functions for DSLs that derive from
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL}.

The factories are ergonomic wrappers, not a separate type system. Their
output is indistinguishable from direct construction and interoperates
with it freely. They are the idiomatic way to build the trees that
traversal (Chapter 12), rewriting (Chapter 13), and the later
expression-template facilities (Chapter 15) consume.
